---
import ReferenceLayout from '../../layouts/ReferenceLayout.astro';
import { SITE_URL } from '../../lib/site.js';

const title = 'Sources and editorial method';
const description =
	'The Sanskrit text of the Kakar Adi Kali Sahasranama and where it comes from: the kakārādi kālī sahasranāma stotram hosted by Sanskrit Documents, its provenance, and how this edition handles transliteration and meaning.';

const schema = {
	'@context': 'https://schema.org',
	'@type': 'WebPage',
	name: title,
	url: `${SITE_URL}/sources/`,
	inLanguage: 'en',
};
---

<ReferenceLayout title={`${title} | Kakar Adi Kali Sahasranama`} description={description} canonical="/sources/" schema={schema} colophonLabel="Sources">
	<div class="page">
		<div class="narrow">
			<nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
				<ol>
					<li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
					<li aria-current="page">Sources</li>
				</ol>
			</nav>

			<p class="page-kicker">Where the words come from</p>
			<h1>Sources and method</h1>

			<section class="landing-section" aria-labelledby="text">
				<h2 class="sec" id="text"><span lang="sa">मूल</span> The source text</h2>
				<div class="prose">
					<p class="drop">
						The thousand names are the <em>kakārādi kālī sahasranāma stotram</em>, also known as the
						<em>kālī medhāsāmrājyaprada sahasranāma</em>. The text itself says it comes from the
						<em>kālakālīkhaṇḍa</em> of the <em>mahākālasaṃhitā</em>.
					</p>
					<p>
						The Sanskrit edition used to check this site is hosted by Sanskrit Documents:
						<a class="u" href="https://sanskritdocuments.org/doc_devii/kaaliisahasra.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
							>sanskritdocuments.org/doc_devii/kaaliisahasra.html ↗</a
						>. That file was proofread by Ravin Bhalekar; the site credits the text to tradition.
					</p>
					<p>
						Its opening line of names reads
						<span lang="sa">ॐ क्रीं काली क्रूँ कराली च कल्याणी कमला कला</span> — the same order as
						positions 1–7 here. The Devanagari on this site was checked against that edition.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="callout">
					<p>
						<strong>Why the verses are not reproduced here.</strong> Sanskrit Documents publishes its
						files for personal study and research, and asks that they not be copied or reposted, and
						that bulk extraction into other sites be avoided. This site therefore links to the edition
						and cites it, but does not republish its Sanskrit. The names, their transliterations and
						the English commentary on these pages are this edition's own.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="landing-section" aria-labelledby="spelling">
				<h2 class="sec" id="spelling"><span lang="sa">वर्ण</span> Spelling and transliteration</h2>
				<div class="prose">
					<p>
						Three forms are kept for every name. The <strong>Devanagari</strong> is the original script.
						The <strong>IAST</strong> is the academic romanization with diacritics — <em>Kālī</em>, not
						<em>Kali</em>. The <strong>source transliteration</strong> is the plain-ASCII form used in the
						source corpus, which is what most readers will have seen elsewhere.
					</p>
					<p>
						Alternate spellings are listed because English renders Sanskrit inconsistently:
						<em>Kaali</em>, <em>Kalii</em> and <em>Kaalee</em> all point at the same name. Search on this
						site folds diacritics, so any of them finds the page.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="landing-section" aria-labelledby="editorial">
				<h2 class="sec" id="editorial"><span lang="sa">टीका</span> Editorial</h2>
				<div class="prose">
					<p>
						The one-line meanings and the longer elaborations are editorial commentary written for this
						edition. They are aids to reading, not translations of a received commentary, and they carry
						no authority beyond that.
					</p>
					<p>
						Corrections are welcome. If a Devanagari form or a transliteration disagrees with the source
						edition, say which name and which position.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="landing-section" aria-labelledby="related">
				<h2 class="sec" id="related"><span lang="sa">संबंध</span> Related</h2>
				<div class="prose">
					<ul>
						<li>
							<a class="u" href="https://jaikhyapaparampara.com/articles/kakaradi-kali-sahasranama-names-1-111-one-line-meanings/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
								>Names 1–111 with one-line meanings</a
							> — the opening section, on the parampara site.
						</li>
						<li>
							<a class="u" href="https://mantrini.jaikhyapaparampara.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MANTRINI</a>
							— the parampara's free japa app, with an Adya Mahakali altar. Offline; the count stays on
							the device.
						</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</section>
		</div>
	</div>
</ReferenceLayout>
